{
  "summary": "This computer needs your attention now. In the past 7 days, three things changed together: Microsoft Defender's real-time protection was switched off, a new account (<account-3>) was created and given administrator rights, and a new automatic task called CacheCleaner appeared that runs a program from a temporary folder. Each could have an innocent explanation. Together they match a pattern attackers often follow, so check them first: turn protection back on, then confirm whether you or someone you trust made these changes.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Defender's real-time protection was turned off this week",
      "finding_ids": [
        "DEF-REALTIME-OFF:6ed445bc",
        "SETTING-WEAKENED:916db687"
      ],
      "explanation": "Microsoft Defender is installed, running and up to date (its virus definitions are 0 days old), but real-time protection is off. That means new malware is not blocked when it arrives. The earlier scan from 7 days ago shows it was on then, so someone or something switched it off since. If you didn't do this on purpose, treat it as a warning sign, especially alongside the other changes in this report. Turning it back on comes first, because it protects the computer while you look into everything else. The fix is shown with each finding in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "A new account with full control was added this week: <account-3>",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-ADMIN:29255107",
        "NEW-USER:8187239c",
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:feb6df5d"
      ],
      "explanation": "These three findings all describe the same account, <computer>\\<account-3>. It did not exist at the scan 7 days ago. It is enabled, it requires a password, and it is an administrator, so it can change anything on this computer. If you or another member of your household created it, this is fine. If nobody did, it could be a way for an outsider to get back in, and you should deal with it urgently. See the suggested fix for each finding in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "New automatic task 'CacheCleaner' runs a program from a temporary folder",
      "finding_ids": [
        "NEW-PERSISTENCE:79b21359",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:658daf42"
      ],
      "explanation": "A scheduled task called CacheCleaner appeared since the last scan. It is set to run a program named cc.exe from your user Temp folder (C:\\Users\\<user>\\AppData\\Local\\Temp). It runs under your own account with limited rights, not as administrator. Many normal apps start themselves automatically, but running from the Temp folder is less usual. It also appeared during the same week as the other changes. If you don't recognise it as something you installed, use the report's fix to look into and remove it."
    },
    {
      "priority": 4,
      "title": "Another administrator account to confirm: <account-2>",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account <computer>\\<account-2> also has administrator rights. It was not flagged as new, so it was already there at the earlier scan. Having a separate administrator account is often good practice, so this is likely normal. Just confirm it's an account you know about."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "NEW-PERSISTENCE:79b21359",
      "suggested_severity": "high",
      "reason": "The program runs from the Temp folder rather than a normal install location. It appeared in the same 7-day window that Defender was turned off and a new administrator was added. That combination makes it more concerning than a typical new startup entry."
    }
  ]
}
